y of health-related issues and dietary habits. It has a notable prevalence of celiac disease, with an incidence ranging from 1 in 85 to 1 in 540 individuals. The continent's dietary patterns reveal that saturated fat consumption primarily comes from meats and dairy products, which contributes to mortality rates associated with cardiovascular diseases (ECV). \n\nIn terms of nutritional health, Europe has seen an increase in the consumption of vitamin B supplements, particularly among individuals facing cognitive challenges. Additionally, it is estimated that between 10,000 and 15,000 people with short bowel syndrome in Europe rely on parenteral nutrition for more than 90 days. The region also has established guidelines for the refeeding of malnourished patients, recommending a caloric intake of 10-15 kcal/kg.\n\nRegulatory frameworks concerning nutritional information for food products are set and enforced across Europe, although there is significant variation in regulations regarding nutritional education and dietary (NED) practices among different countries. The continent also faces health challenges such as cystic fibrosis, with an incidence of 1 in every 3,500 births and a mean prevalence of 0.8 per 10,000 inhabitants. Healthcare spending on diabetes is a major concern, with a substantial portion of health budgets dedicated to managing this condition. Furthermore, Tolvaptán is available in Europe for the treatment of SIADH-related hyponatremia, and Southern Europe has a higher frequency of the IVS10 mutation linked to phenylketonuria.\",11\r\n5609,SAPROPTERINA,\"Sapropterina is a synthetic preparation of tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) that is utilized in the treatment of mild phenylketonuria (PKU). It is often administered alongside dietary management to enhance the patient's ability to tolerate phenylalanine (Phe). As a commercial preparation, Sapropterina plays a crucial role in managing PKU by increasing the tolerance to this amino acid, thereby aiding in the overall treatment strategy for individuals affected by this condition.\",3\r\n7306,CARNE,\"CARNE refers to meat, which plays a significant role in dietary practices. While meat consumption, especially red and processed meat, is associated with various health risks, including chronic diseases, it is also recognized as a vital component of dietary recommendations for children. These recommendations highlight the importance of meat in providing essential protein and maintaining nutritional balance in their diets.\",2\r\n10576,ALIMENTOS LIBRES,Alimentos libres are foods that are low in phenylalanine and can be consumed freely by individuals with phenylketonuria.,1\r\n10575,ALIMENTOS RESTRINGIDOS,Alimentos restringidos are foods that are high in phenylalanine and should be limited in the diet of individuals with phenylketonuria.,1\r\n4996,ALIMENTOS RICOS EN FENILALANINA,\"Foods high in phenylalanine include meats, fish, eggs, cheese, nuts, soy, and aspartame, which must be restricted in the diet of affected individuals.\",1\r\n4999,AMINOÁCIDOS EXENTOS DE FENILALANINA,Commercial preparations of amino acids free of phenylalanine are used for supplementation in patients with phenylketonuria.,1\r\n10536,BH4,Tetrahydrobiopterin is a cofactor necessary for the activity of phenylalanine hydroxylase and is involved in the metabolism of phenylalanine.,1\r\n10537,DHPR,\"Dihydropteridine reductase is an enzyme that plays a role in the regeneration of tetrahydrobiopterin, which is crucial for neurotransmitter synthesis.\",1\r\n10542,DIAGNOSTICO BIOQUIMICO,Biochemical diagnosis of phenylketonuria is established through the measurement of plasma phenylalanine levels.,1\r\n4995,TETRAHIDROBIOPTERINA,\"TETRAHIDROBIOPTERINA, also known as tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4), is a crucial cofactor for the hydroxylases of phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan. It plays a significant role in the treatment of mild phenylketonuria, a metabolic disorder that affects the body's ability to process phenylalani